{"step_index":847,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T23:34:23Z","content":"I will view `tests/test_session_compliance.py` using `view_file` to see how the session compliance test is currently implemented.","thinking":"**Fortifying the Auditor**\n\nI'm focusing on strengthening the auditor's ability to identify anti-patterns, given their current ineffectiveness. The core challenge lies in understanding how these skills are bypassed, and how to create safeguards against this. My thinking is centered around making this an effective, proactive defense rather than a predictable, easily circumvented check.\n\n\n**Strengthening the Safeguards**\n\nMy focus has shifted to the fundamental weakness: passive text-based anti-pattern suggestions are easily ignored. The current approach depends too heavily on LLM memory and attention, lacking the robustness needed. To solve this, I'm developing a solution to make it an active, automated AST linting. Without active scans for string literals, the LLM will circumvent the rules. Therefore, I need to create a test to find the anti-patterns.\n\n\n**Developing Automated Detection**\n\nI'm now implementing the automated AST analyzer. I'm building `scripts/lint_anti_patterns.py` and a corresponding test suite to scan code within `digest/formatters/` and `engine/resolvers/`. This should catch the hardcoded anti-patterns I've identified, and ensure the evidence gate rejects code with them. I'm focusing on detecting static strings, date branches, empty array fallbacks, raw text DB key leaks, and unresolved tone defaults.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"c:\\\\Users\\\\augus\\\\OneDrive\\\\Documents\\\\Google Antigravity\\\\Projects\\\\Typikon Coded\\\\tests\\\\test_session_compliance.py\"","EndLine":"100","StartLine":"1","toolAction":"\"Viewing test_session_compliance.py\"","toolSummary":"\"Viewing session compliance test\""}}]}
