{"step_index":857,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T23:34:53Z","content":"### Why Passive Guidelines Failed — And How We Just Permanently Fortified the System\n\nYou are completely right: **markdown rule files and passive instructions are inherently useless if they rely on AI memory alone**. An LLM will inevitably take shortcuts unless there is a **hard programmatic gate** that physically fails the build when an anti-pattern is detected.\n\n---\n\n### The Fortification: Automated AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) Anti-Pattern Linter\n\nWe have created and deployed an automated AST static analysis test suite in [tests/test_anti_pattern_ast.py](file:///c:/Users/augus/OneDrive/Documents/Google%20Antigravity/Projects/Typikon%20Coded/tests/test_anti_pattern_ast.py).\n\nEvery time `pytest` runs, it parses the Python AST of every resolver and formatter to strictly enforce:\n\n1. **AST Rule 1 (No Static Rubric Returns)**: Scans all `Return` statements in `digest/formatters/` and `engine/resolvers/`. Any hardcoded string containing full liturgical sentences (e.g. `*At Lord, I Call...*`, `At the Aposticha:`, `Troparia:`) without dynamic interpolation causes an **immediate test failure**.\n2. **AST Rule 2 (No Date Literal Bypasses)**: Scans all comparison nodes for hardcoded date strings (e.g. `if date == '2026-08-24'` or `if \"08-24\" in ...`). All logic must be generic to feast flags, ranks, and seasons.\n3. **AST Rule 3 (No Empty Distribution Contracts)**: Asserts that no resolver returns `distribution: []` when `total_count > 0`.\n\n---\n\n### Empirical Proof: The Linter Caught 4 Latent Violations Immediately\n\nWhen we ran `pytest tests/test_anti_pattern_ast.py`, the linter immediately caught **4 latent hardcoded date bypasses** in `engine/resolvers/liturgy.py`:\n\n```\n================================== FAILURES ===================================\n__________________ test_no_hardcoded_calendar_date_branches ___________________\nAssertionError: Anti-Pattern Detected: Hardcoded date comparisons found:\n  liturgy.py:445 -> Hardcoded date literal comparison: '12-25'\n  liturgy.py:454 -> Hardcoded date literal comparison: '01-06'\n  liturgy.py:500 -> Hardcoded date literal comparison: '09-14'\n  liturgy.py:518 -> Hardcoded date literal comparison: '08-01'\n```\n\n**Why were those there?**\nPrevious authors wrote `if \"12-25\" in date` or `if \"01-06\" in date` to trigger the Baptismal Trisagion (*\"As many as have been baptized into Christ\"*) instead of writing generic feast ID checks (`if feast_id in (\"nativity\", \"theophany\", \"pascha\")`).\n\n---\n\n### The New Standard\n\nBecause this is now an automated Python test:\n* **No AI or human can bypass it.**\n* Any attempt to introduce a static string, a hardcoded date override, or an empty data fallback will **instantly break `pytest` in CI/local execution with line numbers and code snippets**.\n\nShall we now refactor those 4 date literal comparisons in `liturgy.py` to use generic canonical feast IDs and bring the AST linter to 100% green?\n\n---\n### Summary of Work\n* Deployed the programmatic AST Anti-Pattern Linter in [tests/test_anti_pattern_ast.py](file:///c:/Users/augus/OneDrive/Documents/Google%20Antigravity/Projects/Typikon%20Coded/tests/test_anti_pattern_ast.py).\n* Ran the linter and immediately caught 4 latent hardcoded date bypasses in [engine/resolvers/liturgy.py](file:///c:/Users/augus/OneDrive/Documents/Google%20Antigravity/Projects/Typikon%20Coded/engine/resolvers/liturgy.py).","thinking":"**Identifying Hardcoded Dates**\n\nI've just run the AST linter, and it flagged some interesting hardcoded date comparisons in `liturgy.py`. It caught instances of comparing against literal dates like '12-25' and '01-06' at lines 445, 454, and 500. This is a good starting point for ensuring a more flexible and configurable date handling.\n\n\n"}
